 archive (recommended).** Label `Vol d'Ambre v3c boosted โ€” pre-correction reference (2026-04-30)`. Keep capped, dark glass, cool. After the main bottle has macerated `10-14 days`, sniff both side-by-side on blotters at `T+0`, `T+30 min`, `T+2 h`, and `T+6 h`. This A/B is the only honest test of whether the fougรจre conversion actually changed the family read or only added top-notes over the same amber base. Without this archive, you cannot answer that question.++2. **Secondary โ€” wear it as a small amber-Layton flanker.** The 3 mL is already at EDP strength. After the main-bottle evaluation is done (and you are sure it succeeded), the archive can be transferred to a `5 mL` atomiser and worn as-is. It will not duplicate `Air-Conditioned Amber Layton` because the post-mix Ambrox/Azarbre/Javanol/Norlimbanol/Timberol boosters made it amber-heavier and woodier than the air-conditioned version.++3. **Tertiary โ€” re-dilute for a lighter wearable.** If 24% raw / 16.6% active is too strong for Thai climate:+   - To EDT strength `~12%` raw / `~8.3%` active: add `3.0 mL` ethanol 96% โ’ final `6 mL` EDT.+   - To cologne strength `~8%` raw / `~5.5%` active: add `6.1 mL` ethanol 96% โ’ final `9.1 mL` cologne. Closer to a typical mass-market splash.+   - Macerate the diluted version `48-72 h` before judging, even though the perfume itself is already integrated. Re-dilution forces the active phase to redistribute through fresh ethanol.++4. **Quaternary โ€” layering base.** Spray `1 puff` of the 3 mL archive on collar/chest, then layer the corrected fougรจre bottle over it. Useful if the corrected main bottle reads too dry/aromatic on certain days and you want to dial amber warmth back in temporarily.++### Things Not To Do++- **Do not discard.** The decant contains `~728 uL` of stock-as-held fragrance and `~498 uL` of active aroma โ€” measurable bench material, not test residue.+- **Do not add the new fougรจre materials to it.** That just makes a smaller version of the corrected bottle and destroys the only reference you have.+- **Do not mix the decant back into the corrected main bottle.** The corrected bottle is already at target `24.0%` raw / `16.62%` active. Adding `3 mL` of `24.29%` stock back would shift the bottle by `~3.4%` toward the original boosted-amber profile and undo a measurable fraction of the fougรจre conversion.+- **Do not store in clear glass on a sunny shelf.** The decant has the same terpene/lavender/limonene oxidation profile as the main bottle. The v3c BHT antioxidant is present but only buys `~12-18 months` of headroom in good storage conditions.++### Storage And Labelling++- Transfer to a clean amber or cobalt `5 mL` glass vial. Headspace under `1 mL` is preferred to limit oxidation surface.+- Label: `Vol d'Ambre v3c boosted โ€” pre-correction reference`, with date `2026-04-30` and concentration `~24% raw / ~16.62% active`.+- Store cool (`<25 ยฐC`), dark, tightly capped.+- Re-cap immediately after each evaluation pull. Each cap-off event during the 10-14 day window adds oxidation pressure; pull on blotters once per evaluation session, not repeatedly.++### Why This Matters For The Decision++The whole reason for the fougรจre conversion was that **`Vol d'Ambre v3c boosted` and `Air-Conditioned Amber Layton` smelled almost the same except for ambroxan level**. The 3 mL archive is the empirical anchor for the question "did we actually fix that?" Without the A/B test against the archive after maceration, the user is judging the corrected bottle against memory, which is unreliable for amber-vanilla-musk profiles where olfactory adaptation is fast.++If the corrected bottle smells clearly different from the archive at `T+0`, `T+30 min`, and `T+2 h` โ€” and especially if it reads more masculine, fresher, and more aromatic at `T+30 min` โ€” the conversion succeeded. If the two read substantially the same after `2 h` of wear, the fougรจre overlay was insufficient and the next move is a small additional `Evernyl + Juniper Berry EO + Lavender EO` push, not another amber/wood adjustment.++---++## Safety And Guardrails++- Do not raise `Evernyl` above `20 uL` in this performance-first version. The old `23 uL` gate-forward dose was legal but too close to the headroom edge and less mass-market.+- Do not add more `Ambrox Super`; the amber band is already the reason this bottle overlaps with Air-Conditioned Amber Layton.+- Do not add `Hedione HC`; it restores the glossy Layton radiance profile and reduces differentiation.+- Do not add `Nagarmortha Oil` in this pass. It is useful, but this formula needs mass-market aromatic freshness, not oud-adjacent darkness.+- `Lime Distilled EO`, `Beta-Pinene`, `Juniper Berry EO`, lavender materials, and petitgrain are terpene-heavy. The v3c BHT antioxidant guard should be present. Store cool, dark, and tightly capped.diff --git a/formulas/collections/Vol_dAurore_Sauvage_Beater_Aromatic_Fougere.md b/formulas/collections/Vol_dAurore_Sauvage_Beater_Aromatic_Fougere.mdnew file mode 100644index 0000000..52a40df--- /dev/null+++ b/formulas/collections/Vol_dAurore_Sauvage_Beater_Aromatic_Fougere.md@@ -0,0 +1,271 @@+# Vol d'Aurore โ€” Aromatic Fougรจre Sublime (Sauvage-Beater Composition)++**Date:** 2026-04-30+**Family:** Modern Aromatic Fougรจre+**Format:** 30 mL EDP, 20% concentration+**Brief:** beat Sauvage EDP on its own market โ€” Thai mass-market masculine โ€” through *art, layering, and texture*, not through cloning. Match Sauvage's strengths (Ambroxan skin warmth, cold-pepper opening, clean diffusion); exceed it on the axes Sauvage neglects (real heart, textural depth, drydown evolution, signature moment).++---++## Why Sauvage Wins, And Where It Leaves Room++| Sauvage strength | The actual mechanism | Why it works for the masses |+| --- | --- | --- |+| Ambroxan skin warmth (~3% neat EDP) | Skin-depot molecule activates with body heat; massive aerial projection | Reads as "expensive" and "masculine" without a complicated brief |+| Cold pepper-bergamot opening | Sichuan pepper + pink pepper + Calabrian bergamot, layered with eugenol | Universal first-impression hit |+| Iso E Super transparent woody | Single material doing structural work; no fat-cedar mass | Modern, easy, doesn't read "old man" |+| Clean white-musk trail | Galaxolide + Habanolide-type macrocyclic musks | Long projection without animalic complexity |++| Sauvage weakness | The actual mechanism | Why it's an opening |+| --- | --- | --- |+| Linear after 30 min | Sauvage skips the heart; goes top โ’ Ambrox slab | A real heart creates surprise and depth |+| Single temperature (cold-clean) | No mineral, no earth, no resin layer | Adding cool-mineral and warm-earth notes creates dimension |+| No emotional warmth | Hedione is present but not pushed; no "smile" molecule load | Pushing Hedione + saffron creates the warmth Sauvage lacks |+| Drydown stagnation | Mostly Ambrox + Iso E for 4 hours | Layered woods create evolution Sauvage cannot match |+| No signature moment | The pepper opening is generic-modern | A textural surprise note (saffron, frankincense bloom) gives the bottle a memory |+| Saturation in market | Every Thai 25-40 man owns it | "Same category but distinctive" beats "more of the same" |++**The strategic conclusion:** do not try to outdo Sauvage at being Sauvage. Beat it by adding the four things Sauvage refuses to do โ€” a real aromatic heart, mineral-incense texture, multi-layer drydown, and a signature surprise โ€” while matching its core (Ambrox + pepper-citrus + Iso E) at parity.++---++## The Four Acts++The bottle is designed as a four-act olfactory architecture, not a three-tier pyramid. Sauvage is a three-tier (top, weak heart, base). Vol d'Aurore is four acts because the *bridge* is the differentiator.++### Act I โ€” The Cold Awakening (0-15 min)++`Bergamot FCF` + `Lime Distilled` + `Cardamom FTEC` + `Eugenol` + `Beta-Pinene` + `Juniper Berry` + `Petitgrain` + `Spike Lavender` (camphoraceous accent).++**What it does:** matches Sauvage's pepper-bergamot opening at parity. The eugenol + cardamom delivers the pepper-bite OR signal; beta-pinene + juniper add the cold-conifer sparkle Sauvage uses pink pepper for. Spike lavender adds a tiny camphoraceous shimmer that signals "aromatic-masculine" in 5 seconds.++**Sauvage parity check:** equal on first impression. The user should not be able to tell they're not smelling Sauvage at minute 2.++### Act II โ€” The Real Heart (15 min - 2 h)++`Lavender EO` + `Linalyl Acetate` + `Clary Sage` + `Geraniol` + `Hedione (massive)` + `Phenethyl Alcohol` + `Ethyl Linalool` + `Cardamom (continuing)`.++**What it does:** this is the act Sauvage skips. Where Sauvage transitions directly from top to Ambroxan base, Vol d'Aurore stops to *show* a fully built aromatic heart. Lavender + clary sage + geranium is the classical aromatic triangle. Hedione at `~8%` of concentrate is the radiance pump โ€” it activates the same OR networks associated with serotonin-pleasure response and gives the bottle its emotional warmth.++**Sauvage parity check:** wins. At minute 30, where Sauvage starts going flat, Vol d'Aurore is at peak heart bloom.++### Act III โ€” The Mineral Bridge (1-3 h)++`Frankincense EO` + `Cypress EO`* + `Vetiver EO` + `Patchouli EO` + `Nagarmortha Oil` (trace).++**What it does:** this is the textural surprise. As the heart fades, a cool-mineral resin layer rises. Frankincense gives the dry-cold incense facet that signals "expensive niche perfumery." Vetiver + patchouli + nagarmortha give an earth shadow underneath. Cypress adds coniferous coolness at the right moment.++**Sauvage parity check:** wins decisively. Sauvage has no equivalent. This is the act that establishes the bottle as *not Sauvage* in a way that reads as *better*, not as *different and worse*.++\* Cypress EO is in V2 (recommended). V1 substitutes additional Petitgrain + Juniper at this position.++### Act IV โ€” The Layered Drydown (3+ h)++`Ambrox Super` (matched to Sauvage at ~2.0% neat) + `Iso E Super` + `Cedarwood Virginia` + `Sandalore` + `Timberol` + `Cashmeran` + `Habanolide` + `Galaxolide` + `Coumarin` (trace) + `Norlimbanol Dextro` (trace).++**What it does:** the Ambrox tier matches Sauvage. The difference is what surrounds it. Sauvage's drydown is mostly Ambrox + Iso E. Vol d'Aurore adds two cedars + sandalwood + Timberol + Cashmeran for a layered woody base where each note reveals at a different VP timing. Coumarin at trace adds tonka warmth without becoming gourmand.++**Sauvage parity check:** parity on Ambrox projection; wins on drydown evolution. At hour 6, where Sauvage is essentially the same as hour 2, Vol d'Aurore is in a different woody-musky-resinous zone.++---++## V1 โ€” The Bench-Buildable Recipe (Use Inventory On Hand)++30 mL EDP, 20% concentration. Concentrate: 6,000 ยตL (= 20% of 30 mL). Ethanol 96%: 24,000 ยตL.++| # | Material | Stock | ยตL | wt% conc | Act | Function |+| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | --- | --- |+| 1 | Ambrox Super | 30% | 2,000 | 33.33 | IV | Skin warmth โ€” Sauvage parity at 2.0% neat |+| 2 | Iso E Super | neat | 540 | 9.00 | IV | Transparent woody architecture |+| 3 | Hedione | neat | 480 | 8.00 | II | Radiance pump โ€” the "smile" molecule |+| 4 | Bergamot FCF | neat | 420 | 7.00 | I | Cold bright citrus top |+| 5 | Lavender EO | neat | 360 | 6.00 | II | Aromatic heart anchor |+| 6 | Cedarwood Virginia | neat | 240 | 4.00 | IV | Dry cedar layer 1 |+| 7 | Linalyl Acetate | neat | 180 | 3.00 | II | Lavender ester โ€” heart body |+| 8 | Habanolide | neat | 180 | 3.00 | IV | Clean macrocyclic musk |+| 9 | Sandalore | neat | 180 | 3.00 | IV | Creamy sandalwood layer |+| 10 | Cardamom FTEC | 10% | 180 | 3.00 | I/II | Spice bridge top โ’ heart |+| 11 | Galaxolide | neat | 180 | 3.00 | IV | Musk anchor |+| 12 | **Frankincense EO** | neat | **120** | **2.00** | **III** | **Mineral-resinous bridge โ€” KEY differentiator** |+| 13 | Clary Sage EO | neat | 120 | 2.00 | II | Herbal-tea sophistication |+| 14 | Petitgrain EO | neat | 90 | 1.50 | I | Bitter green leaf |+| 15 | Geraniol | neat | 90 | 1.50 | II | Geranium freshness |+| 16 | Coumarin | 20% | 90 | 1.50 | IV | Trace tonka warmth (not gourmand) |+| 17 | Phenethyl Alcohol | neat | 60 | 1.00 | II | Soft rose-water heart |+| 18 | Lime Distilled EO | neat | 60 | 1.00 | I | Cold citrus sparkle |+| 19 | **Vetiver EO** | neat | **60** | **1.00** | **III** | **Earth shadow โ€” texture move** |+| 20 | Timberol | neat | 60 | 1.00 | IV | Cedar-sandalwood bridge |+| 21 | Cashmeran | neat | 60 | 1.00 | IV | Soft cedar warmth |+| 22 | Ethyl Linalool | neat | 60 | 1.00 | II | Transparent floral support |+| 23 | Eugenol | neat | 50 | 0.83 | I/II | Pepper-bite proxy (Pink Pepper substitute) |+| 24 | **Patchouli EO** | neat | **30** | **0.50** | **III** | **Earthy depth โ€” texture move** |+| 25 | Spike Lavender EO | neat | 30 | 0.50 | I | Camphoraceous accent |+| 26 | Beta-Pinene | neat | 30 | 0.50 | I | Pine-resin sparkle |+| 27 | Juniper Berry EO | neat | 30 | 0.50 | I | Gin-conifer top |+| 28 | **Nagarmortha Oil** | neat | **12** | **0.20** | **III** | **Cypriol earth-root trace** |+| 29 | Norlimbanol Dextro | neat | 6 | 0.10 | IV | Dry-wood permanence trace |+| โ€” | BHT | neat | 1.2 | 0.02 | โ€” | Antioxidant for terpene stability |+| โ€” | **Total concentrate** | โ€” | **~6,000** | **100.00** | โ€” | โ€” |+| โ€” | Ethanol 96% (bottle fill) | โ€” | 24,000 | โ€” | โ€” | โ€” |+| โ€” | **Final bottle total** | โ€” | **30,000** | โ€” | โ€” | โ€” |++### Neat-Equivalent In Final EDP โ€” Sauvage Parity Check++| Material | wt% in 30 mL EDP (neat) | Sauvage EDP (estimated) | Status |+| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |+| Ambrox Super | 2.00% | 2.5-3.0% | Slightly under but at parity tier |+| Iso E Super | 1.80% | 2.0-3.0% | Parity tier |+| Hedione | 1.60% | <0.5% | **+3ร— Sauvage โ€” emotional warmth** |+| Bergamot FCF | 1.40% | ~1.5% | Parity |+| Lavender EO | 1.20% | ~1.0% | Slightly higher โ€” heart anchored |+| Frankincense EO | 0.40% | 0% | **Vol d'Aurore exclusive** |+| Vetiver EO | 0.20% | 0% | **Vol d'Aurore exclusive** |+| Patchouli EO | 0.10% | trace | Slight edge |+| Coumarin | 0.06% | trace | Parity |+| Eugenol | 0.17% | ~0.10% | Slightly higher โ€” pepper bite |+| Galaxolide + Habanolide | 1.20% | ~1.5% | Parity |++**The numerical story:** Vol d'Aurore matches Sauvage on the core character markers (Ambrox 2%, citrus 1.4%, Iso E 1.8%) and overtakes it where Sauvage is thin (Hedione 3ร— higher, Frankincense + Vetiver + Patchouli where Sauvage has zero, Lavender slightly higher).++---++## V2 โ€” The Ideal Version (Recommended New Material Acquisitions)++V1 is good. V2 is what makes it *exceptional* โ€” what moves the bottle from "great Sauvage alternative" to "art-object aromatic fougรจre."++### Three Materials To Acquire++| Material | Cost (Thai supplier estimate) | Why it elevates Vol d'Aurore |+| --- | --- | --- |+| **Pink Pepper EO** (`Schinus molle` or `Schinus terebinthifolius`) | ~800-1,500 THB / 10 mL | Real pink pepper signal; replaces eugenol-cardamom substitute. Sauvage's actual top-note material. Authentic and distinctive. |+| **Saffron CO2** or **Safranal** | ~2,000-4,000 THB / 5 mL | The signature memorable note no mass-market masculine has. Saffron + lavender is one of the most elegant accords in modern perfumery (used in Penhaligon's Halfeti, Thameen, etc.). This is the *art* differentiator. |+| **Cypress EO** | ~600-1,000 THB / 30 mL | Mineral-coniferous coolness in the bridge act. Adds dimension Sauvage cannot match. Elegant and modern. |++Total acquisition cost: **~3,500-6,500 THB** for materials that will support multiple compositions, not just this one.++### V2 Recipe Adjustments (Replaces V1 Materials)++| Position | V1 | V2 (adjusted) | ฮ” |+| --- | --- | --- | --- |+| Pink Pepper substitute | Eugenol 50 ยตL + extra Cardamom | **Pink Pepper EO 30 ยตL** + Eugenol 18 ยตL + Cardamom 180 ยตL (unchanged) | -32 ยตL net |+| Bridge / texture surprise | (Frankincense + Vetiver + Patchouli + Nagarmortha) | **+ Saffron CO2 / Safranal 8 ยตL** | +8 ยตL |+| Bridge / coniferous cool | (Petitgrain + Juniper carrying this load in V1) | **+ Cypress EO 30 ยตL**, reduce Petitgrain 90 โ’ 60 ยตL | +0 ยตL net |+| Hedione | 480 ยตL | 480 ยตL (unchanged โ€” already at high level) | 0 |+| Volume balancing | โ€” | Reduce Bergamot FCF 420 โ’ 414 ยตL | -6 ยตL |++Net delta: ~+30 ยตL added (Saffron 8 + Pink Pepper 30 + Cypress 30 - Eugenol -32 - Petitgrain -30 + Bergamot -6 = +0 ยตL). Total still 6,000 ยตL.++### What V2 Achieves That V1 Cannot++- **Pink Pepper EO** delivers the *actual* Sauvage-style top note rather than a chemistry-textbook substitute. The eugenol-cardamom proxy is functionally close but olfactorily readable as a substitute by sensitive noses.+- **Saffron CO2** is the *signature*. When a wearer asks "what is that?" โ€” that note is what they're noticing. Sauvage has no such moment. This is the bottle's identity.+- **Cypress EO** in the bridge is what makes the perfume read "expensive niche" rather than "good designer." Cypress is rare in mass-market masculine and instantly elevates the sophistication tier.++If acquiring all three is impractical, **the priority order is: Saffron > Cypress > Pink Pepper**. Saffron is the single highest-impact addition because it gives the bottle a memorable signature.++---++## Mixing Protocol (Both V1 And V2)++### Stage 1 โ€” BHT antioxidant prep++Weigh `~1.2 mg` BHT into a clean 1 mL vial. Add `200 ยตL` ethanol 96%; swirl until fully dissolved. Set aside.++### Stage 2 โ€” Add to concentrate vial in temporal order (slow โ’ fast)++**Group A โ€” Base/fixative tier (Act IV):** add first, allow 5 min between dense additions for distribution.++`Ambrox Super 30%` (2,000) โ’ `Iso E Super` (540) โ’ `Habanolide` (180) โ’ `Galaxolide` (180) โ’ `Sandalore` (180) โ’ `Cedarwood Virginia` (240) โ’ `Timberol` (60) โ’ `Cashmeran` (60) โ’ `Norlimbanol Dextro` (6) โ’ `Coumarin 20%` (90)++**Group B โ€” Bridge tier (Act III):** add second.++`Frankincense EO` (120) โ’ `Vetiver EO` (60) โ’ `Patchouli EO` (30) โ’ `Nagarmortha Oil` (12)+(V2 only: + `Cypress EO` (30) + `Saffron CO2` (8))++**Group C โ€” Heart tier (Act II):** add third.++`Hedione` (480) โ’ `Lavender EO` (360) โ’ `Linalyl Acetate` (180) โ’ `Clary Sage EO` (120) โ’ `Geraniol` (90) โ’ `Phenethyl Alcohol` (60) โ’ `Ethyl Linalool` (60)++**Group D โ€” Top tier (Act I):** add last (most volatile, oxidation-sensitive).++`Bergamot FCF` (420) โ’ `Cardamom FTEC 10%` (180) โ’ `Petitgrain EO` (90) โ’ `Lime Distilled EO` (60) โ’ `Eugenol` (50) โ’ `Spike Lavender EO` (30) โ’ `Beta-Pinene` (30) โ’ `Juniper Berry EO` (30)+(V2 only: + `Pink Pepper EO` (30), reduce Eugenol to 18, reduce Petitgrain to 60)++**BHT pre-solution:** add as final step before sealing.++### Stage 3 โ€” Seal and macerate concentrate++Cap, invert `15ร—` gently. Macerate concentrate `7 days` before transferring to the 30 mL bottle. **The concentrate maceration is non-negotiable for this composition.** The four-act structure requires the materials to cross-condition before dilution; skipping this step results in a flatter final bottle.++### Stage 4 โ€” Bottle and final maceration++Transfer concentrate to the 30 mL bottle. Add `24,000 ยตL` ethanol 96%. Cap, invert `20ร—` gently.++**Macerate `8 weeks minimum` before final evaluation.**++This is longer than the typical 4 weeks because:+- Frankincense + Vetiver + Patchouli need extended integration time with the woody base.+- Saffron (V2) develops over 6+ weeks; judging early misses the actual character.+- Hedione + Lavender + Clary Sage cross-conditioning takes 3-4 weeks alone.++### Evaluation Schedule++- **Week 1:** check direction only. Family read = aromatic fougรจre? If yes, on track.+- **Week 4:** check Act II and III bloom. The heart should be present and distinct from Sauvage by here.+- **Week 8:** verdict. Compare side-by-side with a 5 mL travel of Sauvage EDP on blotters at T+0, T+30 min, T+2 h, T+6 h, T+12 h.+- **Week 12:** final character locked. Make any small final adjustments only after this point.++---++## What "Beating Sauvage" Looks Like At Week 8++Side-by-side blotter comparison checklist:++| Time | Sauvage | Vol d'Aurore (target) | Vol d'Aurore wins if... |+| --- | --- | --- | --- |+| T+0 (spray) | sharp pepper-bergamot-Ambrox punch | sharp pepper-bergamot-Ambrox punch with subtle cardamom warmth | parity, no clear winner |+| T+15 min | pepper fading, Ambrox rising | pepper fading, lavender-clary heart starting to bloom | Vol d'Aurore reads "more dimensional" |+| T+30 min | Ambrox dominant, heart minimal | full aromatic heart in bloom: lavender + sage + geranium + Hedione radiance | Vol d'Aurore reads as a *fuller perfume* โ€” clear win |+| T+1 h | Ambrox + Iso E flat plateau | mineral bridge rising: frankincense + vetiver + cedar emerging | Vol d'Aurore reveals new facets โ€” clear win |+| T+2 h | Ambrox + Iso E unchanged | layered woody-musky drydown developing | Vol d'Aurore continues to evolve |+| T+6 h | Ambrox + faint Iso E | woody-musky-resinous skin scent with Coumarin warmth | Vol d'Aurore reads more sophisticated |+| T+12 h | Ambrox skin trace | woody-musky skin trace with subtle resinous cool | Vol d'Aurore retains dimensionality |++**The win condition is not "smells better."** It is: at every time point past T+30 min, Vol d'Aurore reveals more facets, more dimensions, more *intention* than Sauvage. The user should be able to point to *what* the bottle is doing at any time โ€” a specific note in bloom, a specific transition happening. Sauvage cannot be described that way past minute 30; it is just "Sauvage." That is the difference between a well-engineered functional accord and a crafted composition.++---++## On The Question Of Beating Sauvage In Thai Mass Market++The honest commercial answer: a single bench-blended bottle does not beat a global marketing machine. Sauvage's market position is held by Johnny Depp + Dior distribution + duty-free saturation, not by olfactory superiority.++What this composition *can* do:++1. **Beat Sauvage on the noses of people who care.** Perfume enthusiasts, fragrance bloggers, sensitive wearers. Word-of-mouth tier.+2. **Beat Sauvage in the niche/artisanal segment.** Positioned at 2,500-4,000 THB / 30 mL with proper packaging, this composition is a credible competitor to Lattafa Asad, Maison Alhambra masculine line, and the upper Armaf tier.+3. **Beat Sauvage on the discerning gift-buyer.** The "she wants to give him something more sophisticated than Sauvage" buyer is a real segment in Bangkok and Chiang Mai mid-luxury retail.++What it cannot do without marketing infrastructure:++- Beat Sauvage on volume.+- Beat Sauvage on the unaware mass-market consumer.+- Beat Sauvage on the impulse-buyer at duty-free.++This is fine. The brief was *beat Sauvage on its market through art*. Art wins on the discerning end of any market. This composition is engineered to do that.++---++## Estimated Cost Per 30 mL Bottle (V2 Including Saffron + Cypress + Pink Pepper)++| Cost component | Estimate (THB) |+| --- | ---: |+| Raw materials (V2) | 450-600 |+| Ethanol 96% (24 mL) | 30 |+| Bottle + atomiser + box (premium) | 200-400 |+| Label and brand work (one-time amortised) | 50-100 |+| BHT, transfer pipettes, etc. | 20 |+| **Total cost basis** | **~750-1,150 THB** |++At a sale price of 3,500-4,500 THB / 30 mL (correctly positioned premium niche), gross margin is **70-80%** โ€” appropriate for the artisanal niche segment.++V1 (without Saffron + Cypress + Pink Pepper) costs ~`100-200 THB` less per bottle but loses the signature character that justifies the premium price tier. V2 is the version that justifies the positioning.diff --git a/formulas/collections/premium_mass_4000_thb_universal_eighteen_ppm_odt_audit_2026_05_02.md b/formulas/collections/premium_mass_4000_thb_universal_eighteen_ppm_odt_audit_2026_05_02.mdnew file mode 100644index 0000000..227ad6e--- /dev/null+++ b/formulas/collections/premium_mass_4000_